  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Open-thread membership derives from `IMembershipResolverService` (ADR-034) on the thread's ADR-024 anchor; 1:1 uses the explicit two-participant list; private adds the explicit per-record grant.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A reconcile job (on the existing Service Bus job contract) reconciles ACS thread participants (Add/Remove) to the derived set, triggered event-driven AND by a periodic sweep.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A Dataverse access change reconciles ACS membership within the sweep window — verified by test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">ACS membership NEVER exceeds the Dataverse-derived authorized set (projection invariant) — verified by an over-exposure guard test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">An audit entry is recorded per membership change (participant, thread, trigger, timestamp, actor).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Reconcile is best-effort/non-fatal (NFR-02): a simulated failure does NOT fail send/capture; it retries + DLQs; the sweep repairs drift.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`dotnet build` clean; publish-size delta reported vs ~45.30 MB; no new HIGH CVE; Step 9.5 gates run; `/conflict-check` clean.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Depends on 040 (thread identity + anchor) + 011 (ACS membership ops). Parallel-safe with 042/043 after 040 lands. Blocks nothing directly, but the projection invariant it enforces is what makes 042's privacy enforcement trustworthy at the transport layer — keep them consistent.

    Eventual consistency is expected (design §8.4). The sweep is the safety net; do not attempt strong consistency between Dataverse and ACS. Threads &gt;20 participants lose ACS read-receipts/typing (fine for R1; note for R2). If ADR-034 derivation seems insufficient for a private-thread edge case, surface it via the §6.5 ADR-conflict protocol rather than inventing a second ACL.
  </notes>

